  "description": "The local reattach handshake across a core restart (immortality §6.1, LOCKED: SO_PEERCRED alone, no claim token; the §6.3 sketch with every token field stripped). Run 1 opens a session and runs its first handshake. The core is killed; run 2 is a new core process, which reattaches the connection instead of reconnecting it: the worker's first event to it is link_state, the core resends the timer values, dials the session's data socket and sends hello with last_committed_seq — the last output frame or gap marker its recording keeps. The worker answers on the new data peer: its ring holds frames 4 to 9 (first_buffered_offset counts the output bytes before frame 4). The core asks for everything after 5; the worker writes 6 to 9 with their original seq and stamp, then replay_done through 9, its current_seq, since from hello until live it reads nothing from the channel. Only after live does the worker resume output and accept input. A capture of a real run must show this order in every run.",
